{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>The angel who redeems, etc.</b> Meaning: the utterance of HaShem that HaShem commands to His beloved is called by the name 'angel,' for the voice of HaShem hews flames of fire (Psalms 29:7). And this is his intent in saying 'the angel,' etc. — meaning, that the memra of HaShem be in their support and bless them." "<b>And let my name be called upon them, etc.</b> Meaning: that they will be in the gradation of the three patriarchs, and just as no person in the world can sever or corrupt [the spiritual level of] one of the three patriarchs, and needless to say all of them together, so too will they be." "<b>Further,</b> it intends the language of honor and greatness, meaning that through their agency honor will reach the name of the patriarchs. And this is along the lines of what He says (Proverbs 23:24): ״גִּיל יָגִיל אֲבִי צַדִּיק״, and so He says (Proverbs 17:6): ״עֲטֶרֶת זְקֵנִים בְּנֵי בָנִים״, and this will come about through the agency of their fitting preparation and upright way, until honor reaches the fathers of their fathers." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes that the mention of their name before HaShem will be cherished by Him like the mention of the name of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. And so you will find that the prophet said (Jeremiah 31:19): ״הֲבֵן יַקִּיר לִי אֶפְרַיִם וְגוֹ׳ כִּי מִדֵּי דַבְּרִי בּוֹ״ — see how the name of Ephraim became precious in the eyes of HaShem, and He mentions it with great desire and affection:" "<b>And let them swarm into a multitude, etc.</b> The intent is along the lines of what I explained in parashat Bereshit at the verse (Genesis 1:22): ״וַיְבָרֶךְ אוֹתָם״, etc., which is said of the fish — that HaShem needed to bless them because their dwelling place is opposite, at the far end, to the aspect of birth; therefore He amplified in them the power of generation to reproduce even in the water. And here Jacob said that they should swarm like fish in abundance of birth, and this fence [i.e., boundary of reproductive power] will be theirs upon the earth. Accordingly, their blessing will be greater than the blessing of the fish who are in the water — and understand this." "There is a fine point to examine in his saying <b>in the midst</b> — an allusion that they will be hidden away within, after the manner of what Joshua told them (Sotah 36b) when the blessing of Jacob was fulfilled in them: ״Go hide yourselves in the woods,״ for Joshua explained the verse according to our interpretation. And there is a midrash on this (Berakhot 55b) that he likened them to fish, over which the evil eye has no dominion; accordingly, his saying <b>in the midst of the earth</b> means: even though they are in the midst of the earth — and in this respect they are in a category superior to fish."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:19.516366996Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}